         <h2 id="what-is-eeat">🔍 What Is E-E-A-T for AI Priority?</h2>

         <p><strong>E-E-A-T for AI Priority</strong> is Google's quality evaluation framework that determines which pages get cited inside AI-generated search summaries. The acronym stands for Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, and Trustworthiness. Google's human quality raters use a 160-page guidelines document to train the machine learning systems that make these decisions automatically at scale.</p>

         <p>The framework originated as E-A-T in Google's first public guidelines in 2014. Google added the first E — Experience — in December 2022, recognising that first-hand practical experience is distinct from academic expertise. This matters enormously for AI Priority: a page written by someone who has actually done the thing they are describing outscores a page written by someone who has only researched it. According to <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2022/12/google-raters-guidelines-e-e-a-t" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Search Central's December 2022 announcement</a>, this change specifically targeted the problem of highly-optimised but experientially hollow content.</p>

         <p>For AI Overviews specifically, E-E-A-T functions as a pre-filter. Before Google's AI system even considers citing your page, it checks whether the page passes minimum thresholds on all four pillars. A page that scores high on Expertise but low on Trustworthiness — because it lacks citations or HTTPS — will not be selected. You need all four. The practical implication: fixing your weakest E-E-A-T pillar often unlocks AI citation eligibility faster than improving your strongest one.</p>

         <blockquote>
            "The key question is whether the content demonstrates that it was produced with some degree of experience, such as with actual use of a product, having actually visited a place, or communicating what a person experienced. There is a difference between expertise and experience."
            <cite>— <strong>Google Search Quality Evaluator Guidelines</strong>, Section 3.2 — What is E-E-A-T? (<a href="https://static.googleusercontent.com/media/guidelines.raterhub.com/en//searchqualityevaluatorguidelines.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google, 2024</a>)</cite>
         </blockquote>

