         <h2 id="four-pillars">📊 The Four Pillars: How Each Affects AI Citations</h2>

         <p>Understanding each pillar's specific role in <strong>E-E-A-T for AI Priority</strong> lets you allocate your optimisation effort correctly. Not all four pillars carry equal weight for every content type. YMYL (Your Money or Your Life) pages — health, finance, legal — face stricter evaluation on Expertise and Trustworthiness. How-to and tutorial content is evaluated more heavily on Experience.</p>

         <p><strong>Experience</strong> is demonstrated through first-person case studies, original data, and documented real-world outcomes. A legal firm that publishes anonymised client case studies with specific outcomes scores higher on Experience than one that only publishes academic summaries of legislation. <strong>Expertise</strong> requires named authors with verifiable credentials — a job title, LinkedIn profile, professional body membership, or published work that Google can cross-reference. <strong>Authoritativeness</strong> is primarily a site-level signal driven by backlink quality, brand mentions in reputable publications, and topical authority. <strong>Trustworthiness</strong> covers accuracy, transparency, and technical trust signals: cited sources, HTTPS, clear contact information, and no deceptive content practices.</p>

         <p>Implementing all four requires a structured approach. Start with Trustworthiness — it is the most technically verifiable and the easiest to fix. Add HTTPS, update your about page with real contact details, and add citations to every statistic. Then address Expertise by adding a real author bio with credentials. Experience and Authoritativeness take longer because they require original content and external validation respectively. According to <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/article" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's Structured Data documentation</a>, Article schema with Author and Person markup directly signals Expertise and Authoritativeness to automated systems.</p>

         <blockquote>
            "For the most helpful content, the author's name is visible and the content aligns with that author's demonstrated area of expertise. Anonymously authored content gets less benefit of the doubt from our systems."
            <cite>— <strong>Google Search Central Blog</strong>, "More guidance on building high quality sites" (<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2023/04/quality-site-review" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google, 2023</a>)</cite>
         </blockquote>

