         </div>

         <p>Understanding the AI Overview selection process helps you optimise in the right sequence. Google does not simply take the top-ranking page for a query and cite it. The selection process for <strong>E-E-A-T for AI Priority</strong> involves multiple evaluation stages, and E-E-A-T filtering happens before relevance matching.</p>

         <p>The process works in four stages. First, Google identifies a pool of potentially relevant pages for the query — typically several hundred candidates. Second, it applies E-E-A-T filters to eliminate pages that fail minimum quality thresholds. Third, it evaluates the remaining pages for relevance and query match. Fourth, it synthesises content from the selected sources into the AI Overview response. Your page can be perfectly relevant and technically optimal, but if it is filtered out in stage two, it will never appear in stage four. This is why <strong>E-E-A-T for AI Priority</strong> optimisation must come before relevance optimisation for pages targeting AI citation.</p>

         <p>The practical signals Google uses in stage two include: presence of a named author with verifiable credentials, Article or BlogPosting schema markup, at least one outbound citation to a reputable external source, HTTPS, and absence of deceptive design patterns. According to <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's Helpful Content guidance</a>, pages that exist primarily to attract search engine traffic — rather than to serve human readers — are systematically downweighted in AI source selection.</p>

         <blockquote>
            "We don't want to show AI Overviews that contain information that could be harmful to people. We use our existing quality signals — including E-E-A-T — to help determine which content is appropriate to use."
            <cite>— <strong>Liz Reid</strong>, VP of Search, Google (<a href="https://blog.google/products/search/generative-ai-google-search-may-2024/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Blog, May 2024</a>)</cite>
         </blockquote>

         <p><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Check whether your page has Article schema with an Author node pointing to a Person entity with a URL. This single change is the most consistently impactful technical improvement for E-E-A-T for AI Priority across all the sites ContentScale has audited.</p>

