         <h2 id="graaf-eeat">⚡ The GRAAF Framework and E-E-A-T: Direct Mapping</h2>

         <p>ContentScale's <strong>GRAAF Framework</strong> was designed to score E-E-A-T for AI Priority signals in a deterministic, measurable way. The five GRAAF pillars — Genuinely Credible, Relevant, Actionable, Accurate, Fresh — map directly onto Google's four E-E-A-T dimensions. This makes ContentScore a practical proxy for your AI Priority eligibility.</p>

         <p>Genuinely Credible (G) covers the Expertise and Authoritativeness dimensions: named authors with credentials, expert citations, case studies, and verifiable claims. Relevant (R) maps to the Experience dimension: content that demonstrates first-hand knowledge of the topic, appropriate keyword focus, and match with search intent. Accurate (A) and Fresh (F) together cover the Trustworthiness dimension: dated statistics, cited sources, current information, and no factual errors. Actionable (A) does not have a direct E-E-A-T equivalent but improves the page's value signal — pages that give users something concrete to do are preferred by both human raters and AI selection systems. Using the <a href="https://app.contentscale.site">free ContentScale scanner</a>, you can see your score on all five GRAAF pillars and identify which E-E-A-T dimension needs the most work.</p>

         <p>The 100-point ContentScore breaks down as: GRAAF 50 points, CRAFT 30 points (content quality), Technical SEO 20 points. For E-E-A-T for AI Priority specifically, the GRAAF score is the most important. Pages scoring 90+ on ContentScore — which requires a GRAAF score of approximately 45/50 — consistently demonstrate all four E-E-A-T pillars at a level that qualifies for AI Overview citation. Pages scoring 70–89 have gaps in one or two pillars. Pages below 70 typically fail E-E-A-T filtering entirely.</p>

         <div class="warning-box">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold mb-2 text-yellow-400">⚠️ Common Mistake: Optimising for Keyword Density Before E-E-A-T</h3>
            <p class="mb-0">Many SEO practitioners focus on keyword placement and backlink building before addressing E-E-A-T signals. For AI Priority specifically, this is counterproductive. A perfectly keyword-optimised page without a named author and cited sources will be filtered out of AI Overview selection before relevance is even evaluated. Fix E-E-A-T first, then optimise for relevance.</p>
         </div>

         <blockquote>
            "Our systems automatically try to surface helpful, reliable information. E-E-A-T is not a ranking factor in the traditional sense — it is a quality signal used across many of our systems to understand whether a page is a reliable source."
            <cite>— <strong>Google Search Central</strong>, "Understanding E-E-A-T" (<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google, 2025</a>)</cite>
         </blockquote>

         <p><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> After improving your E-E-A-T signals, rescan your page on <a href="https://app.contentscale.site">ContentScale</a> and compare your GRAAF sub-scores. The Genuinely Credible sub-score is your best proxy for overall E-E-A-T for AI Priority readiness.</p>

